<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 803–<a href="/years/814">814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Krum the Fearsome, Khan Krum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krum was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> from about 803 to 814. He destroyed the remnants of the Avar Khaganate in the north-west and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Serdica</a> in 809. In 811 he trapped and killed the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a>, reportedly turning his skull into a drinking cup, and in 813 he defeated Michael I and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. He is also credited with the first written Bulgarian laws.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krum.jpg" alt="Krum" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manasias · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krum33.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The eastern Avar lands were probably taken by the First Bulgarian Empire under <b>Khan Krum</b> early in the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After killing the emperor Nikephoros I in 811, <b>Khan Krum</b> had the emperor's skull lined with silver and used it as a drinking cup, according to Byzantine sources.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Byzantines had burned the Bulgar capital Pliska, <b>Khan Krum</b>'s forces trapped their army in a pass of the Balkan Mountains and destroyed almost all of it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-26">26 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Khan Krum</b> of Bulgaria routed the Byzantine army of Emperor Michael I Rangabe near Versinikia, not far from Adrianople in Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-22">22 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
